license: MIT
# Third-party test data: tests/python/meshes/exodus/DCBmodel_PD_solid.e is a
# PeriLab reference output, Copyright (c) 2023 Christian Willberg and
# Jan-Timo Hesse (DLR), redistributed unmodified under BSD-3-Clause with its
# notice and licence text alongside it. BSD-3-Clause is permissive and imposes
# no obligation on the rest of this repository. See that directory's README.md.
#
# Third-party test data: tests/python/meshes/pcd/*.pcd are files from the Point Cloud
# Library's own test corpus (https://github.com/PointCloudLibrary/pcl, test/),
# Copyright (c) 2009-2012 Willow Garage, Inc. and (c) 2012- Open Perception, Inc.,
# redistributed unmodified under the BSD-3-Clause licence, whose text sits beside them as
# LICENSE.PCL. BSD-3-Clause is permissive and imposes no obligation on the rest of this
# repository. The `pcd` reader/writer (src/cpp/src/formats/pcd.cpp, src/python/
# meshioplusplus/pcd/) implements the published PCD v0.7 layout; its LZF codec is an
# independent implementation of the liblzf stream format, and no liblzf code is copied.
#
# Third-party test data: tests/python/meshes/nastran_h5/*.h5 are MSC Nastran 2020 result
# files from the pyNastran repository (https://github.com/SteveDoyle2/pyNastran, models/),
# Copyright (c) 2011-2026 Steven Doyle, redistributed unmodified under the BSD-3-Clause
# licence, whose text sits beside them in that directory's README.md. pynastran_reference.npz
# there is pyNastran's own reading of them, frozen by tools/gen_nastran_h5_reference.py.
# BSD-3-Clause is permissive and imposes no obligation on the rest of this repository. The
# `nastran_h5` reader reads the published MSC schema; no pyNastran code is copied.
# tests/python/meshes/nastran/composite_plate_2022.fem is an OptiStruct deck from the same
# repository (models/optistruct/), redistributed unmodified under the same BSD-3-Clause
# licence, whose text sits beside it in that directory's README.md.
# tests/python/meshes/ansys/*.cdb are Ansys MAPDL decks from the mapdl-archive repository
# (https://github.com/akaszynski/mapdl-archive), Copyright (c) 2023-2024 Alex Kaszynski,
# redistributed unmodified under the MIT licence, whose text sits beside them in that
# directory's README.md; mapdl_archive_reference.npz there is mapdl-archive's own reading
# of them, frozen by tools/gen_ansys_cdb_reference.py. The `ansysInp` reader's element
# categories and degenerate-shape rules follow mapdl-archive's; no code is copied.
# tests/python/meshes/ansys/rst/*.rst, *.rth and dist_static/*.rst are MAPDL result files from the
# pymapdl-reader repository (https://github.com/ansys/pymapdl-reader), Copyright (c)
# 2021-2026 Synopsys, Inc. and ANSYS, Inc., redistributed unmodified under the MIT
# licence, whose text sits beside them in that directory's README.md;
# pymapdl_reference.npz there is pymapdl-reader's own reading of them, frozen by
# tools/gen_ansys_rst_reference.py. The `ansys_rst` reader reads the record layout
# pymapdl-reader documents; no code is copied.
# tests/python/meshes/mfem/*.mesh are sample meshes from the data/ directory of MFEM
# (https://github.com/mfem/mfem), Copyright (c) 2010-2026 Lawrence Livermore National
# Security, LLC, redistributed unmodified under the BSD-3-Clause licence, whose text sits
# beside them in that directory's README.md; the *.gf grid functions and reference.npz
# there were written by MFEM itself (PyMFEM) through tools/gen_mfem_fixtures.py. The
# `mfem` reader reproduces MFEM's documented degree-of-freedom numbering; no MFEM code is
# copied.
# tests/python/meshes/femap/*.neu are Femap neutral files from FrontISTR
# (https://github.com/FrontISTR/FrontISTR, Copyright (c) 2019 FrontISTR Commons),
# ems_file_format_converter (https://github.com/EMSolution-SSIL/ems_file_format_converter,
# Copyright (c) 2025 Science Solutions International Laboratory, Inc.) and
# femap_neutral_parser (https://framagit.org/numenic/femap_neutral_parser, Copyright (c)
# 2021 Nicolas Cordier), all MIT, redistributed (some concatenated) by
# tools/gen_femap_fixtures.py with the licence text beside them in that directory's
# README.md. The `femap` reader's record layouts follow FrontISTR's neu2fstr and those
# files; no code is copied.
# tests/python/meshes/abaqus_fil/pybaqus/*.fil are Abaqus 2023 results files from the test
# suite of pybaqus (https://github.com/cristobaltapia/pybaqus, Copyright (c) 2020
# Cristóbal Tapia Camú, MIT), redistributed unmodified with the licence text beside them
# in that directory's README.md; no code is copied.
# tests/python/meshes/z88/cantilever/z88o2.txt and z88o3.txt were written by the Z88R
# solver of Z88OS V15 (https://github.com/LSCAD/Z88OS) for the deck
# tools/gen_z88_fixtures.py writes; no Z88OS file or code is included.
#
# One exception to the MIT licence above: the Julia binding in bindings/julia/
# is released under GPL-3.0 (copyleft: no permission needed to use/modify/
# sell it, but distributing it or a modified version must stay GPL-3.0 with
# source available; private use carries no obligation). The C++ core, the C
# API it calls and the R binding in bindings/r/ are all MIT. See
# bindings/julia/LICENSE.
#
# The `cae` format's on-disk key layout (src/python/meshioplusplus/cae/) and
# the `usd` writer's stage layout (src/python/meshioplusplus/usd/) are
# transcribed from the LAYOUTS written by the Kratos Multiphysics
# PhysicsNeMoApplication (CaeDatasetExportProcess and deployment/usd_export,
# BSD-4-Clause with an advertising clause, Copyright (c) 2026 Vicente Mataix
# Ferrandiz -- the same author as this project). No Kratos-typed code is
# copied: both are re-implementations over meshio++'s own Mesh, cell blocks
# and operations. The `pmsh` and `zarr` formats target NVIDIA PhysicsNeMo's
# own on-disk layouts (Apache-2.0), written here in pure numpy from the
# observed file structure and pinned against upstream's own save/load in
# both directions. See doc/formats/cae.md, doc/formats/usd.md,
# doc/formats/pmsh.md and doc/formats/zarr.md.
#
# The isotropic clustering engine behind the `remesh` operation
# (src/cpp/src/operations/remesh.cpp) is derived from pyacvd
# (https://github.com/pyvista/pyacvd), MIT License, Copyright (c) 2017-2024
# The PyVista Developers -- itself an independent implementation of the
# published research of S. Valette and J.-M. Chassery, not of the CeCILL-B
# licensed ACVD project (https://github.com/valette/ACVD), which this
# codebase never reads code from and never vendors. remesh's optional
# quadric-driven ("feature-preserving") metric has no pyacvd counterpart and
# is an original synthesis built on this project's own pre-existing
# Garland-Heckbert quadric machinery (detail/decimate_common.hpp). See
# doc/remesh.md for the full attribution and licence reasoning.
#
# The `remesh_volume` operation (src/cpp/src/operations/remesh_volume.cpp)
# is implemented from the PUBLISHED DESCRIPTION only of:
#   J. Labelle and J. R. Shewchuk, "Isosurface Stuffing: Fast Tetrahedral
#   Meshes with Good Dihedral Angles", ACM Transactions on Graphics
#   (SIGGRAPH 2007), 26(3), Article 57.
# Neither the paper's own reference implementation (Stellar, non-commercial
# licence) nor TetGen (AGPL-3.0, also by Shewchuk) is read or vendored here.
# The 12-tet body-centered-cubic lattice tetrahedralization used is this
# project's own derivation (a deliberately simpler alternative to the
# paper's own construction), independently verified and algebraically
# pinned rather than transcribed. See doc/remesh_volume.md for the full
# attribution and licence reasoning.
#
# SmoothMethod::Odt (src/cpp/src/operations/smooth.cpp) cites the
# variational-tetrahedral-meshing literature (e.g. P. Alliez et al.,
# "Variational Tetrahedral Meshing", SIGGRAPH 2005) for the optimal-Delaunay-
# triangulation energy it minimizes; CGAL's GPL-licensed Mesh_3
# implementation is not read.
#
# The `optimize_volume` operation (src/cpp/src/operations/optimize_volume.cpp)
# implements the local-mesh-improvement-by-swapping rule from the PUBLISHED
# DESCRIPTION only of:
#   L. A. Freitag and C. Ollivier-Gooch, "Tetrahedral mesh improvement using
#   swapping and smoothing", International Journal for Numerical Methods in
#   Engineering, 40(21), 1997, pp. 3979-4002.
# A topological flip (2-3 / 3-2) is applied only when it strictly raises the
# worst incident element quality, using signed-volume tests alone -- no
# in-sphere / orientation predicate kernel, and no external mesh library, is
# read or vendored. See doc/optimize_volume.md.
#
# The `gid` writer (src/cpp/src/formats/gid.cpp) is built on a vendored
# hardcopy of gidpost 2.14 (https://www.gidsimulation.com/), CIMNE's C
# library for writing GiD postprocess files, at
# src/cpp/third_party/gidpost/. Copyright (c) 2015-2024 CIMNE
# (www.cimne.com), released under BSD-2-Clause-Views -- permissive, and
# imposes no obligation on the rest of this repository beyond keeping the
# licence notice with the vendored code (see that directory's LICENSE and
# README.meshioplusplus.md, which also records what was excluded and why,
# most notably gidpost's own Fortran binding and its `cfortran.h`-dependent
# file, which carries a separate third-party distribution policy of its
# own and is not vendored here).
